# Documentation and Handoff

## When to Update Docs

Update docs in the same work unit when:
- Architecture boundaries change (`docs/architecture/`)
- Infrastructure setup changes (`docs/infrastructure/`)
- A new public interface is added or changed
- An incident is resolved (`docs/operations/incident-response.md`, `.sdlc/memory/incidents.md`)
- A major stage transition happens (`docs/handoff/current-state.md`)

## Docs Structure

| Directory                  | Purpose                                  |
|----------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| `docs/architecture/`       | System design, decisions, context        |
| `docs/infrastructure/`     | Infra, local dev, deployment             |
| `docs/handoff/`            | State summaries and transition records   |
| `docs/roadmap/`            | Planned work and timeline                |
| `docs/operations/`         | Observability, incidents, maintenance    |
| `.sdlc/process/`           | SDLC process authority                   |

## Docs Quality

- No empty files. Every doc must have useful content.
- Use tables for structured data (stage lists, integration status, etc.).
- Use headers to make docs scannable.
- Keep docs honest — no aspirational statements presented as current state.
- Mark sections as "TBD" or "placeholder" when content is not yet defined.

## Handoff Format

Every handoff document must include:
1. What changed
2. Why it changed
3. What was validated
4. Known risks or open questions
5. Next steps
6. Rollback instructions (if applicable)

## ADR Format

Architecture decisions go in `docs/architecture/decisions.md`:
```
## ADR-NNN — <title>

- **Date:** YYYY-MM-DD
- **Status:** proposed | accepted | superseded
- **Context:** Why the decision was needed.
- **Decision:** What was decided.
- **Consequences:** What this means going forward.
```
